---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfToWord from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/PdfToWord.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="pdf-to-word">
  <PdfToWord client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert a PDF to Word</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop the PDF onto the box above. pdf.js reads it locally, and nothing is uploaded.</li>
      <li>Let the text extraction finish. You will see the word, paragraph and heading counts as soon as it is done.</li>
      <li>Decide whether to detect headings and whether to keep one page break per PDF page.</li>
      <li>Check the preview. It shows the exact structure the document will have in Word.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Create .docx</strong> and download the file.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What is preserved, and what is not</h2>
    <p>
      Being straight about this matters more than the conversion itself. A PDF is a
      description of marks on a page; a Word document is a flowing structure of
      paragraphs and styles. Turning one into the other is a reconstruction, not a
      translation, and every converter that promises perfect fidelity is
      overselling.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Element</th>
            <th>Result</th>
            <th>Why</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Body text</td>
            <td>Preserved, in reading order</td>
            <td>Extracted directly from the PDF's text layer</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Paragraph breaks</td>
            <td>Preserved</td>
            <td>Inferred from line spacing and indentation</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Headings</td>
            <td>Detected heuristically, styled as Heading 1 / 2</td>
            <td>PDFs carry no structure, so line shape is the only signal</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Page breaks</td>
            <td>Optional, one per PDF page</td>
            <td>Page boundaries are known exactly</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Bold, italic, colour, font sizes</td>
            <td>Not preserved</td>
            <td>Requires mapping every embedded font back to a style</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Tables</td>
            <td>Flattened to text</td>
            <td>PDFs draw table borders as lines, with no cell structure</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Images and charts</td>
            <td>Not included</td>
            <td>The text layer is extracted; graphics are separate objects</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Columns, headers, footers</td>
            <td>Flow into the body text</td>
            <td>They are just text at particular coordinates</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Scanned pages</td>
            <td>Not convertible</td>
            <td>There are no characters to extract. OCR is required</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      So this PDF to Word tool is excellent when you want the words in an editable
      form: a report to revise, a letter to reuse, a policy to quote from.
      It is the wrong tool if you need a Word file that looks exactly like the PDF.
      For that, find the original document.
    </p>

    <h2>How heading detection works</h2>
    <p>
      Since the PDF carries no outline, the converter judges each extracted
      paragraph by its shape. A line becomes a heading when it is short (under
      about ninety characters and twelve words), does not end in a full stop,
      comma, semicolon or colon, and matches one of these patterns:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>a numbered section prefix such as <code>3.</code> or <code>3.2</code> (the deeper the numbering, the lower the heading level);</li>
      <li>a run of capitals, as in <code>TERMS AND CONDITIONS</code>;</li>
      <li>an opening word like <em>Chapter</em>, <em>Section</em>, <em>Part</em> or <em>Appendix</em>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The result is a document whose navigation pane and automatic table of
      contents actually work. If the heuristic misfires on your document, turn it
      off and style the headings yourself. It is one click in Word.
    </p>

    <h2>Is the .docx a genuine Word file?</h2>
    <p>
      Yes. A <code>.docx</code> is a ZIP archive containing an Open Packaging
      Conventions structure, and this tool writes the whole thing: the content-type
      manifest, the package relationships, the main document part, a stylesheet
      defining Normal, Body Text and the three heading styles, the document's own
      relationships, and both sets of document properties. It is not a renamed
      HTML file or an RTF, so Word, LibreOffice Writer, Apple Pages and Google Docs
      all open it natively and let you edit and re-save normally.
    </p>

    <h2>Why doing this locally matters</h2>
    <p>
      The documents people convert to Word are contracts, invoices, medical
      letters, tax returns and legal filings. An online converter uploads them to a
      server and promises to delete them later. Here, pdf.js extracts the text and
      JSZip builds the ZIP, both in this browser tab. There is no page limit, and
      the tool works with your network disconnected, which is the easiest way to
      verify that nothing is uploaded.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
